MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Backlog items #5, #11 and #3.  Four SEV1/SEV2 harness tests go from red to
green; no test changes anywhere in this commit.

Item #5 — live scoring never persisted partner_name.
The backlog prescribed "one field on the insert: copy the claimed partner
string".  Measured against the real 2026 mirror, that prescription is wrong
twice over.

  Wrong VALUE.  scoring_engine._pair_key_for is
  frozenset((competitor_name, partner_name)), and competitor_name is written
  as comp.display_name at every insert site.  ProCompetitor.display_name is
  the bare name, so pro's two conventions coincide and nobody has had to
  choose before.  CollegeCompetitor.display_name is "Nell Horgan (FVC-A)",
  while the claimed strings in college_competitors.partners are bare
  mixed-case first names ("Teagan", "GREER", "MATEO").  Writing the claim
  verbatim yields {"Greer Swoboda (MSU-A)", "Teagan"} against
  {"Teagan Wigen (MSU-A)", "GREER"}, which are never equal.  The fix would
  appear to land and change nothing.

  Wrong BRANCH.  All eight rows of college Double Buck already exist at
  status='pending', and existing_results_for_event filters on event, id and
  type but NOT on status, so every one takes the UPDATE path.  The insert
  branch is never reached for the population the bug is filed against.

So partner_name is now derived on BOTH paths, resolved through the app's own
three-tier matcher to the partner's display_name.  That makes the row
internally consistent with competitor_name and collapses the pair with zero
changes to scoring_engine, the highest-risk code in the app.

The pool is event-enrolled, not heat-local, and the difference is not
marginal.  Measured across all eight partnered events, 146 claims:
heat-local resolves 80, event-enrolled resolves 145 (144 reciprocal).  Two
causes.  Peavey Log Roll, Pulp Toss and Partnered Axe Throw carry 53 claims
between them and have no heats generated at all.  And two of twenty-four pro
Double Buck pairs are split across heats.

The derivation is guarded on a truthy result, so a resolution failure leaves
a good imported value alone rather than blanking it.

Item #11 — heat undo then re-entry lost partner_name.
Fixed for free by the above: the undo deletes the result rows, re-entry
recreates them, and the derivation re-reads the competitor's partners JSON
against a pool that unions heat membership with existing result rows and so
survives the deletion.

Item #3 — scratch undo did not restore heat membership.
execute_cascade strips the competitor out of every non-completed heat,
mutating the competitors JSON, the stand_assignments JSON and the
HeatAssignment rows.  None of it was captured in the snapshot and none of it
was restored by reverse_cascade, so an undo handed back a competitor who was
active, scored and paid, and on no heat sheet anywhere.  On race day that is
a competitor who never gets called to the stand.

The snapshot now records heat id, position within the running order, and the
stand, captured before each mutation.  The undo restores by position rather
than appending, since the competitors JSON is the order the judge sheet
prints.  It calls sync_assignments so the HeatAssignment rows realign, and it
re-runs rebalance_stock_saw_solo_stands over the restored events: the scratch
left a solo Stock Saw heat and the rebalance moved the survivor onto the
alternating stand, so restoring the original stand can otherwise land two
competitors on the same one.  The rebalance forces a pair back onto 7 and 8
in comp order, which repairs exactly that, and early-returns for every event
that is not Stock Saw.

Partner-name invariant, widened.
scratch_cascade was already internally inconsistent: :143 looks the partner's
row up by competitor_name == partner_name (display_name), while :119 and :350
compare partner_name to the bare competitor.name.  It only worked because
pro's conventions coincide and college partner_name was 100% NULL.  Both
comparisons now match either form.  Every pro row ever written satisfies both,
so nothing about pro changes.

Verification, all with PROAM_APP_ROOT and PROAM_RIG_TEMPLATE set to the
post-Phase-0 mirror:

  proam_regression   26 tests, 15 failed / 11 passed
                     (main baseline: 19 failed / 7 passed; exactly four
                      flipped red to green, none flipped green to red)
  tests/ minus route_smoke
                     3201 tests, 10 failed, byte-identical to the same 10 on
                     clean main at 95aed55 (nine test_mark_assignment, one
                     test_identity_migration; both pre-existing, neither
                     touched here)
  negative control   reverting only scratch_cascade.py puts
                     test_scratch_undo_restores_heat_membership back to red
                     with heats [418, 429, 459, 469] lost
  ruff               clean on both files
